 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Opacity Peeling](8428-opacity-peeling.md)
-Opacity peeling is an interaction technique that reveals internal volumetric structures by discarding the first $n$ layers of tissue along each ray before contributing to the final image. Unlike view-independent methods such as [Brain Peeling](13890-brain-peeling.md) which rely on precomputed distance fields, opacity peeling is view-dependent and operates by accumulating alpha values until specific thresholds are met, allowing users to interactively select which occluded layer to visualize.
+Opacity peeling is an interaction technique that reveals internal volumetric structures by discarding the first $n$ layers of tissue along each ray before contributing to the final image. Unlike view-independent methods such as [Brain Peeling](13890-brain-peeling.md) which rely on precomputed distance fields, opacity peeling is view-dependent and operates by accumulating alpha values until specific thresholds are met, allowing users to interactively select which occluded layer to visualize. Choose this variant when volumetric transparency and alpha blending are required rather than discrete surface layer skipping [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The complexity is dominated by the ray traversal and intersection counting for each pixel. Peeling adds a simple integer comparison per intersection, which is negligible compared to the intersection search itself.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires an additional integer buffer of size $W \times H$ for the peeling buffer [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides intuitive, view-dependent exploration of internal structures without precomputing global distance fields. Simple to implement on top of existing isosurface renderers.
-*   **Limitations:** Only exposes discrete layers; does not provide a smooth transition between layers. Performance is similar to standard isosurface rendering, as all intersections must be found to count them accurately.
-*   **When to use:** When users need to quickly inspect specific internal layers of a volume without removing outer data physically or using complex clipping volumes.
